---
// Commentary: "One shared core, many wrappers". Decision 4 (Swann, 1 October 2026): the page and
// its race chart stay, with every date corrected in place to what the Atlas's own sourced records
// hold, and each migration date drawn by its kind and its bindingness instead of as a hard
// deadline.
//
// The migration half of the chart is read at build from the Atlas's records: the lines of each
// place's profile timeline as the profile itself shows them (readTimeline in
// src/lib/site/evidence.ts, over the JSON copy in data/profiles) and the dated targets of the
// guides a place issues, which for the EU are the targets of the EU coordinated roadmap as
// readiness.json records them (datesFor in src/lib/site/dates.ts). A profile line that a confirmed
// annotation folds into a guide is drawn once, as the guide's row, as on Target dates. Each mark
// carries the kind and bindingness its record holds; what rests on an annotation still awaiting
// confirmation is drawn as "not recorded". The profile lines are not taken from datesFor alone
// because it holds the United States' lines back from production until they are re-read at the
// primary source (REREAD, a condition of the Target dates comparison), while the United States
// profile shows them and the prose names them. The chart therefore cannot drift from the
// profiles; the dates written in the prose are checked against the same marks at build
// (PROSE_DATES, with a warning) and by a test in src/lib/site/changelog.test.ts.
//
// The qubit half shows figures the companies have announced. They are not Atlas records, so
// each was read at its source in this session (1 October 2026), as were the citations below:
//   IBM, https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-roadmap-2033 (4 Dec 2023):
//     "We have introduced IBM Condor, a 1,121 superconducting qubit quantum processor based on our
//     cross-resonance gate technology."
//   IBM, https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/100k-qubit-supercomputer (21 May 2023):
//     "100,000 connected qubits is an achievable goal by 2033."
//   Microsoft, https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/blog/quantum/2025/02/19/microsoft-unveils-majorana-1-the-worlds-first-quantum-processor-powered-by-topological-qubits/
//     (19 Feb 2025): "And we've already placed eight topological qubits on a chip designed to house one million."
//   Amazon, https://www.amazon.science/blog/amazon-announces-ocelot-quantum-chip (27 Feb 2025):
//     five cat data qubits and four ancilla qubits, nine in all.
//   Gidney, https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15917 (21 May 2025): "I estimate that a 2048 bit RSA
//     integer could be factored in less than a week by a quantum computer with less than a million
//     noisy qubits."
//   Gidney and Ekerå, https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09749 (23 May 2019): "How to factor 2048 bit RSA
//     integers in 8 hours using 20 million noisy qubits".
//   G7 Cyber Expert Group, https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy2609 (25 Sep 2024).
//   Global Risk Institute, https://globalriskinstitute.org/publication/2024-quantum-threat-timeline-report/
//     (6 Dec 2024): "combines insights from 32 global experts".
//   Budde and Konrad, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2019.01.007: "Tentative governing of fuel
//     cell innovation in a dynamic network of expectations", Research Policy 48(5), 2019.
// The other dates on the page come from Atlas records: FIPS 203 (data/results/USA.json, 13 August
// 2024); the Cyber Resilience Act (data/lab/rulebook/acts.json, the rest of the Regulation applies
// from 11 December 2027); the NIS Cooperation Group roadmap (2025, data/profiles/EUU.json);
// Executive Orders 14412 (22 June 2026) and 14306 (2025) (data/results/USA.json); the DST task
// force report (2026, data/results/IND.json); the joint position on quantum key distribution
// (2024, January in its address, data/results/NLD.json).
import { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DataTable from '../components/lab/DataTable.astro';
import { datesFor, foldLines } from '../lib/site/dates';
import type { DateRow } from '../lib/site/dates';
import { readTimeline } from '../lib/site/evidence';
import { isEuMember, nameInSentence, profilesByIso3, placeName } from '../lib/site/joins';
import { projectRoot } from '../lib/lab/load';
import { INSTRUMENT_STATUS_META } from '../lib/regulation';
import { pageHref } from '../lib/site/gates';
import { GITHUB_URL, ZENODO_DOI, ZENODO_URL } from '../lib/site/config';
import datesCopy from '../../data/lab/dates/copy.json';

const copy = datesCopy.copy as Record<string, any>;

// ---- the migration dates, from the Atlas's records -------------------------------------------

const ACTORS = ['DEU', 'EUU', 'USA', 'IND', 'THA', 'CHN'];
const profiles = profilesByIso3();

/** One dated line of a place's own record, as the chart, the table and the description use it. */
interface Mark {
  iso3: string;
  year: number;
  month: number | null;
  display: string | null; // the year as the record writes it, when it is a range
  label: string;
  kind: DateRow['kind'];
  bindingness: DateRow['bindingness'];
  source: string; // the record it is read from
}

// the profile's migration timeline as the JSON copy holds it
const rawTimeline = (iso3: string): string | null => {
  const file = join(projectRoot(), 'data', 'profiles', `${iso3}.json`);
  if (!existsSync(file)) return null;
  const raw = JSON.parse(readFileSync(file, 'utf8')) as { migrationTimeline?: unknown };
  return typeof raw.migrationTimeline === 'string' ? raw.migrationTimeline : null;
};

function marksOf(iso3: string, name: string): Mark[] {
  // the profile's own lines, as its profile shows them; a line folded into a guide is drawn as the guide's row
  const lines = readTimeline(iso3, rawTimeline(iso3), isEuMember(iso3)).filter(
    (l): l is typeof l & { year: number } => typeof l.year === 'number',
  );
  const folded = foldLines(iso3, lines.map((l) => ({ year: l.year, label: l.label })));
  const own: Mark[] = lines
    .filter((l, i) => !folded[i] && !l.open)
    .map((l) => ({
      iso3,
      year: l.year,
      month: null,
      display: l.display !== String(l.year) ? l.display : null,
      label: l.label,
      // what rests on an annotation awaiting confirmation is not drawn: the line reads not recorded
      kind: l.lead ? null : l.kind,
      bindingness: l.lead ? null : ((l.bindingness as DateRow['bindingness']) ?? null),
      source: `${name} profile`,
    }));
  // the dated targets of the guides the place issues itself (the EU coordinated roadmap, for the EU)
  const issued: Mark[] = datesFor([iso3])
    .filter((r) => r.relation === 'own' && r.origin === 'guide' && !r.lead)
    .map((r) => ({
      iso3,
      year: r.year,
      month: r.month,
      display: r.display ?? null,
      label: r.label,
      kind: r.kind,
      bindingness: r.bindingness,
      source: r.issuer ? `${r.issuer}${r.guideTitle ? `, ${r.guideTitle}` : ''}` : `${name} profile`,
    }));
  return [...own, ...issued].sort((a, b) => a.year - b.year || (a.month ?? 13) - (b.month ?? 13));
}

type Lane = 'law' | 'guidance' | 'none';
const laneOf = (b: DateRow['bindingness']): Lane => {
  if (!b) return 'none';
  return INSTRUMENT_STATUS_META[b]?.binding ? 'law' : 'guidance';
};
const LANE_HEAD: Record<Lane, string> = {
  law: 'Set in law',
  guidance: 'Set in guidance',
  none: 'Bindingness not recorded',
};
const kindKey = (k: DateRow['kind']) => k ?? 'none';
const kindWord = (k: DateRow['kind']) => (copy.kindWord as Record<string, string>)[kindKey(k)];
const kindLabel = (k: DateRow['kind']) => (copy.kind as Record<string, string>)[kindKey(k)];
const kindNameOf = (k: string) => (copy.kind as Record<string, string>)[k] ?? k;
const howSet = (r: Mark) => {
  const lane = laneOf(r.bindingness);
  if (lane === 'none') return 'Bindingness not recorded';
  const b = INSTRUMENT_STATUS_META[r.bindingness!]?.label ?? r.bindingness!;
  return `${LANE_HEAD[lane]} (${b.toLowerCase()})`;
};

const actors = ACTORS.map((iso3) => {
  const p = profiles.get(iso3);
  const name = p?.country ?? placeName(iso3);
  return { iso3, name, posture: p?.posture ?? null, rows: marksOf(iso3, name) };
});
const rows = actors.flatMap((a) => a.rows);
const withDates = actors.filter((a) => a.rows.length > 0);
const withoutDates = actors.filter((a) => a.rows.length === 0);
// the coordination posture of a place in words, so colour is never the only carrier of it
const postureWords = (a: (typeof actors)[number]) => a.posture?.short ?? 'no coordination posture recorded';

// The dates the prose names, each checked against the marks above when the page is built. A
// profile edit that removes one prints a warning in the build log and fails the test that reads
// this literal (src/lib/site/changelog.test.ts); the prose is then Swann's to correct, and the
// chart and table already follow the record.
const PROSE_DATES: Record<string, number[]> = { DEU: [2030, 2031, 2035], EUU: [2030, 2035], USA: [2030, 2031, 2035], IND: [2029, 2033], THA: [2028, 2035] };
for (const [iso3, years] of Object.entries(PROSE_DATES)) {
  const have = new Set(rows.filter((r) => r.iso3 === iso3).map((r) => r.year));
  const missing = years.filter((y) => !have.has(y));
  if (missing.length) console.warn(`[commentary] the prose names ${missing.join(', ')} for ${iso3}, which its record no longer holds`);
}
if (withoutDates.some((a) => a.iso3 !== 'CHN') || !withoutDates.some((a) => a.iso3 === 'CHN')) {
  console.warn('[commentary] the prose says the Atlas records no migration date for China; check that sentence against the record');
}

// ---- the chart --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const YMIN = 2023;
const YMAX = 2040;
const X0 = 150;
const X1 = 700;
const x = (yr: number) => X0 + ((yr - YMIN) / (YMAX - YMIN)) * (X1 - X0);
const ticks = [2024, 2026, 2028, 2030, 2032, 2034, 2036, 2038, 2040];

// Top panel: physical qubits on a log scale.
const QT = 52; // y at 10^7
const QB = 248; // y at 10^0
const qy = (q: number) => QB - (Math.log10(q) / 7) * (QB - QT);
const decades = [
  { q: 10000000, t: '10M' }, { q: 1000000, t: '1M' }, { q: 100000, t: '100k' },
  { q: 10000, t: '10k' }, { q: 1000, t: '1k' }, { q: 100, t: '100' }, { q: 10, t: '10' },
];
// dLab is the announced chip, tLab the stated target; ly and tly nudge those labels vertically and
// mx nudges a marker sideways, so the clustered low-qubit points do not collide.
const firms = [
  { demo: { yr: 2023, q: 1121 }, dLab: 'IBM Condor, 1,121 (2023)', ly: 14,
    target: { yr: 2033, q: 100000 }, tLab: 'IBM target, 100,000 by 2033', tly: -9 },
  { demo: { yr: 2025, q: 8 }, dLab: 'Microsoft Majorana 1, 8 (2025)', ly: -10 },
  { demo: { yr: 2025, q: 9 }, dLab: 'Amazon Ocelot, 9 (2025)', mx: 16, ly: 18 },
];

// Bottom panel: one group per way a date is set, one row per place in it, the places without a
// recorded date last. The rows are grouped, never ranked: places keep the order of ACTORS.
const LANES: Lane[] = ['law', 'guidance', 'none'];
const B0 = 286; // the panel title
const RH = 28; // a place's row
const GH = 26; // a group's head
type ChartRow = { y: number; name: string; color: string | null; marks: Mark[] };
type ChartGroup = { y: number; head: string; rows: ChartRow[] };
const groups: ChartGroup[] = [];
let cy = B0 + 22;
for (const lane of LANES) {
  const inLane = withDates
    .map((a) => ({ a, marks: a.rows.filter((r) => laneOf(r.bindingness) === lane) }))
    .filter((e) => e.marks.length > 0);
  if (!inLane.length) continue;
  const g: ChartGroup = { y: cy, head: LANE_HEAD[lane], rows: [] };
  cy += GH;
  for (const { a, marks } of inLane) {
    g.rows.push({ y: cy, name: a.name, color: a.posture?.color ?? null, marks });
    cy += RH;
  }
  groups.push(g);
  cy += 6;
}
const noneGroup = withoutDates.length
  ? { y: cy, head: 'No date recorded', rows: withoutDates.map((a, i) => ({ y: cy + GH + i * RH, name: a.name, color: a.posture?.color ?? null })) }
  : null;
if (noneGroup) cy += GH + withoutDates.length * RH;
const AXIS_Y = cy + 4;
const H = AXIS_Y + 28;

// the shape of a mark gives its kind of date, as on the Target dates view
function shape(kind: string, cx: number, cyy: number): { tag: 'circle' | 'rect' | 'polygon'; attrs: Record<string, number | string> } {
  switch (kind) {
    case 'plan':
      return { tag: 'circle', attrs: { cx, cy: cyy, r: 5 } };
    case 'priority':
      return { tag: 'polygon', attrs: { points: `${cx},${cyy - 6} ${cx + 6},${cyy} ${cx},${cyy + 6} ${cx - 6},${cyy}` } };
    case 'complete':
      return { tag: 'rect', attrs: { x: cx - 4.5, y: cyy - 4.5, width: 9, height: 9 } };
    case 'procurement':
      return { tag: 'polygon', attrs: { points: `${cx},${cyy - 6} ${cx + 6},${cyy + 4.5} ${cx - 6},${cyy + 4.5}` } };
    case 'other':
      return { tag: 'rect', attrs: { x: cx - 6, y: cyy - 2.25, width: 12, height: 4.5 } };
    default:
      return { tag: 'rect', attrs: { x: cx - 1.75, y: cyy - 6.5, width: 3.5, height: 13 } };
  }
}
// the coordination postures of the places drawn, for the key: colour beside a name means only this
const postures = [...new Map(actors.filter((a) => a.posture).map((a) => [a.posture!.key, a.posture!])).values()];
const kindsShown = [...new Set(rows.map((r) => kindKey(r.kind)))].sort(
  (a, b) => ['plan', 'priority', 'complete', 'procurement', 'other', 'none'].indexOf(a) - ['plan', 'priority', 'complete', 'procurement', 'other', 'none'].indexOf(b),
);

// ---- words drawn from the same rows ---------------------------------------------------------------

const joinAnd = (xs: string[]) => (xs.length < 2 ? xs.join('') : `${xs.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${xs[xs.length - 1]}`);
const yearsOf = (a: (typeof actors)[number]) => joinAnd([...new Set(a.rows.map((r) => String(r.year)))]);
const placeLine = (a: (typeof actors)[number]) => {
  const lanes = [...new Set(a.rows.map((r) => howSet(r).toLowerCase()))];
  const dates = a.rows.some((r) => r.kind) ? joinAnd(a.rows.map((r) => `${r.year} (${kindWord(r.kind)})`)) : yearsOf(a);
  return `${a.name} (${postureWords(a)}): ${dates}, ${joinAnd(lanes)}`;
};
const noDateSentence = withoutDates.length
  ? `The Atlas records no migration date for ${joinAnd(withoutDates.map((a) => nameInSentence(a.name)))}.`
  : '';
const sentenceCase = (t: string) => t.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + t.slice(1);
const chartDesc = [
  `One ${YMIN} to ${YMAX} timeline. Above, qubit counts the companies have announced and IBM's stated target of 100,000 connected qubits by 2033, against a 2025 estimate that breaking RSA-2048 would take under a million noisy qubits.`,
  `Below, the migration dates the Atlas records, each place with its coordination posture: ${withDates.map(placeLine).join('. ')}.`,
  withoutDates.length ? `${joinAnd(withoutDates.map((a) => `${a.name} (${postureWords(a)})`))}: no date recorded.` : '',
].join(' ');

const tableRows = withDates.flatMap((a) =>
  a.rows.map((r) => ({
    place: a.name,
    posture: sentenceCase(postureWords(a)),
    year: r.month ? `${['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June', 'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December'][r.month - 1]} ${r.year}` : String(r.display ?? r.year),
    label: sentenceCase(r.label),
    kind: kindLabel(r.kind),
    set: howSet(r),
    source: r.source,
  })),
);
const tableColumns = [
  { key: 'place', label: 'Place' },
  { key: 'posture', label: 'Coordination posture' },
  { key: 'year', label: 'Date' },
  { key: 'label', label: 'What the record says' },
  { key: 'kind', label: 'Kind of date' },
  { key: 'set', label: 'How it is set' },
  { key: 'source', label: 'Record' },
];

const methodology = pageHref('/methodology');
const methodDates = methodology ? `${methodology}#dates` : null;
const profileHref = (iso3: string) => pageHref(`/countries/${iso3.toLowerCase()}`);
---

<Base
  title="One shared core, many wrappers"
  description="A QSC Atlas commentary on why the post-quantum transition is fragmenting, and how it is governed all the same, with every migration date drawn as the Atlas records it."
>
  <article class="cm">
    <header class="cm-head">
      <h1 class="cm-h1">One shared core, many wrappers</h1>
      <p class="cm-standfirst">Why the post-quantum transition is fragmenting, and how it is governed all the same.</p>
      {
        methodology && (
          <p class="cm-meta">
            A commentary from the QSC Atlas. <a href={methodology}>How the Atlas classifies countries</a>
          </p>
        )
      }
    </header>

    <p class="prose">
      One set of algorithms now sits underneath almost every national plan for post-quantum cryptography. When a United
      States federal agency published three standards in August 2024, a contested question became a settled technical
      fact, and within months those choices were in European certification catalogues, in national guidance from Paris
      to Bonn, and in the browsers shipping to the world. The core is shared. What surrounds it is not.
    </p>

    <h2>The wrappers: an instrument cascade</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      The same algorithms arrive wrapped in very different instruments. In the European Union they are becoming binding
      through NIS2 and DORA and, from December 2027, the Cyber Resilience Act, though rarely by name. The obligation is
      carried by an undefined phrase, state of the art, whose cryptographic content is supplied by an interpretive layer
      of certification guidance that sits between legislation and practice. Elsewhere the same core is wrapped in soft
      national roadmaps, and across much of the world in nothing at all.
    </p>

    <h2>The clocks diverge, against an uncertain threat</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      The timetables fragment as sharply as the instruments, and every one of them is set against a threat that cannot
      be dated. Germany's BSI, in its technical guideline, sets 2030 for highly sensitive applications, 2031 for
      classical asymmetric encryption used on its own and 2035 for classical signatures. The EU coordinated roadmap, a
      recommendation to Member States, sets 2030 for high-risk use cases and 2035 for medium-risk ones. A United States
      executive order of June 2026 sets 2030 and 2031 for federal high value assets and high-impact systems, and
      national security systems are to complete their move to CNSA 2.0 by 2035. India's Department of Science and
      Technology names 2029 for critical information infrastructure and 2033 for enterprise-wide adoption. Thailand's
      cyber agency runs a phased roadmap that moves high-priority systems by 2028 and completes in 2035. The Atlas
      records no national migration date for China.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      What each clock races is not an observed danger but an expectation. Climate governance can point to rising
      temperatures; quantum-safe governance has no such observable, only the qubit counts the industry says it will
      reach and an uncertain threshold at which they break today's encryption. The gap between the two is the whole
      risk, and no calendar settles it: a 2025 estimate (Gidney) puts a machine able to break RSA-2048 at under a
      million noisy qubits, while the largest target any roadmap in the chart below states is 100,000 connected
      qubits by 2033. The dates do their work anyway, because expectations are performative: they cascade vertically, a
      revised hardware estimate registering at once in Washington's gates and Brussels' targets, and they align
      horizontally, as when four national agencies co-signed a single position in January 2024 that confined quantum
      key distribution to niche uses and named post-quantum migration the priority. Each national clock is, in the
      end, a wager that migration will finish before the machine arrives.
    </p>

    <figure class="race" aria-labelledby="race-cap">
      <p class="race-hint" aria-hidden="true">Drag sideways to see the full chart</p>
      <div class="race-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The quantum-safe race, a chart that scrolls sideways">
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 720 ${H}`} role="img" aria-labelledby="r-t r-d" class="race-svg">
          <title id="r-t">The quantum-safe race: qubit counts against the migration dates the Atlas records</title>
          <desc id="r-d">{chartDesc}</desc>

          {
            ticks.map((t) => (
              <g>
                <line x1={x(t)} y1="48" x2={x(t)} y2={AXIS_Y} class="race-grid" />
                <text x={x(t)} y={AXIS_Y + 18} class="race-year" text-anchor="middle">
                  {t}
                </text>
              </g>
            ))
          }

          {/* top panel: the quantum clock */}
          <text x="8" y="24" class="race-panel">The quantum clock: where industry says its qubit counts are heading</text>
          {
            decades.map((d) => (
              <g>
                <line x1={X0} y1={qy(d.q)} x2={X1} y2={qy(d.q)} class="race-grid faint" />
                <text x={X0 - 8} y={qy(d.q) + 3} class="race-axis" text-anchor="end">
                  {d.t}
                </text>
              </g>
            ))
          }
          <line x1={X0} y1={qy(1000000)} x2={X1} y2={qy(1000000)} class="race-crqc" />
          <text x={X1} y={qy(1000000) - 6} class="race-note" text-anchor="end">
            Under a million noisy qubits: estimate to break RSA-2048 (Gidney, 2025)
          </text>
          {
            firms.map((f) => (
              <g>
                {f.target && <line x1={x(f.demo.yr)} y1={qy(f.demo.q)} x2={x(f.target.yr)} y2={qy(f.target.q)} class="race-traj" />}
                <circle cx={x(f.demo.yr) + (f.mx || 0)} cy={qy(f.demo.q)} r="4.5" class="race-demo" />
                <text x={x(f.demo.yr) + (f.mx || 0) + 11} y={qy(f.demo.q) + 3 + (f.ly || 0)} class="race-label">
                  {f.dLab}
                </text>
                {f.target && <circle cx={x(f.target.yr)} cy={qy(f.target.q)} r="5" class="race-target" />}
                {f.target && (
                  <text x={x(f.target.yr) + 9} y={qy(f.target.q) + 3 + (f.tly || 0)} class="race-label">
                    {f.tLab}
                  </text>
                )}
              </g>
            ))
          }

          {/* bottom panel: the migration dates, grouped by how each is set */}
          <text x="8" y={B0} class="race-panel">The migration dates the Atlas records, by how each is set</text>
          {
            groups.map((g) => (
              <g>
                <text x="8" y={g.y + 14} class="race-group">
                  {g.head}
                </text>
                <line x1="8" y1={g.y + 19} x2={X1} y2={g.y + 19} class="race-rule" />
                {g.rows.map((r) => (
                  <g>
                    {r.color ? (
                      <circle cx="13" cy={r.y + 16} r="4" style={`fill:${r.color}`} />
                    ) : (
                      <circle cx="13" cy={r.y + 16} r="3.5" class="race-dot-none" />
                    )}
                    <text x="23" y={r.y + 20} class="race-name">
                      {r.name}
                    </text>
                    <line x1={x(Math.min(...r.marks.map((m) => m.year)))} y1={r.y + 16} x2={x(Math.max(...r.marks.map((m) => m.year)))} y2={r.y + 16} class="race-span" />
                    {r.marks.map((m) => {
                      const s = shape(kindKey(m.kind), x(m.year), r.y + 16);
                      const Tag = s.tag;
                      return (
                        <g>
                          <Tag {...s.attrs} class="race-mark" />
                          <text x={x(m.year)} y={r.y + 5} class="race-y" text-anchor="middle">
                            {m.year}
                          </text>
                        </g>
                      );
                    })}
                  </g>
                ))}
              </g>
            ))
          }
          {
            noneGroup && (
              <g>
                <text x="8" y={noneGroup.y + 14} class="race-group">
                  {noneGroup.head}
                </text>
                <line x1="8" y1={noneGroup.y + 19} x2={X1} y2={noneGroup.y + 19} class="race-rule" />
                {noneGroup.rows.map((r) => (
                  <g>
                    {r.color ? (
                      <circle cx="13" cy={r.y + 12} r="4" style={`fill:${r.color}`} />
                    ) : (
                      <circle cx="13" cy={r.y + 12} r="3.5" class="race-dot-none" />
                    )}
                    <text x="23" y={r.y + 16} class="race-name">
                      {r.name}
                    </text>
                    <text x={X0} y={r.y + 16} class="race-none">
                      No migration date in its profile
                    </text>
                  </g>
                ))}
              </g>
            )
          }
          <line x1={X0} y1={AXIS_Y} x2={X1} y2={AXIS_Y} class="race-axisline" />
        </svg>
      </div>

      <dl class="race-key">
        <div>
          <dt>Kind of date, the shape of a mark:</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              {
                kindsShown.map((k) => {
                  const s = shape(k, 8, 8);
                  const Tag = s.tag;
                  return (
                    <li>
                      <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                        <Tag {...s.attrs} class="race-mark" />
                      </svg>
                      {kindNameOf(k)}
                    </li>
                  );
                })
              }
            </ul>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Coordination posture, the dot beside a name:</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              {
                postures.map((m) => (
                  <li>
                    <span class="race-key-dot" style={`background:${m.color}`} aria-hidden="true" />
                    {m.short}
                  </li>
                ))
              }
              {
                actors.some((a) => !a.posture) && (
                  <li>
                    <span class="race-key-dot race-key-dot--none" aria-hidden="true" />
                    No coordination posture recorded
                  </li>
                )
              }
            </ul>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <figcaption id="race-cap">
        The quantum-safe race. Above, qubit counts the companies have announced and IBM's stated target, against a 2025
        published estimate of what breaking RSA-2048 would take. Below, the migration dates the Atlas records for{' '}
        {joinAnd(withDates.map((a) => nameInSentence(a.name)))}, grouped by whether the Atlas records them as set in law or
        set in guidance, or has not recorded their bindingness; the shape of each mark gives its kind of date where the
        Atlas has recorded it.{' '}
        {withoutDates.length > 0 && `${noDateSentence} `}
        A target is not a legal deadline unless a binding instrument sets it.{' '}
        {methodDates && (
          <>
            <a href={methodDates}>How the Atlas labels dates</a>.
          </>
        )}
      </figcaption>

      <DataTable
        caption="The migration dates in the chart, by place"
        columns={tableColumns}
        rows={tableRows}
      />
    </figure>

    <h2>The failure mode is congestion, not absence</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      A network of expectations can fail, and its failure mode is congestion rather than a missing authority. The
      financial sector already faces DORA, national supervisors, G7 guidance and sectoral roadmaps at once, and the
      result is deferral: ENISA finds only a small share of European stakeholders have begun to migrate. The answer is
      not necessarily a central authority. It is to make the state of the transition common knowledge, who stands where,
      on which instrument, to what clock.
    </p>

    <p class="prose cm-close">
      One shared core, many wrappers, held loosely together by a network of expectations. The Atlas maps the wrappers,
      country by country, so the fragmentation can at least be read.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      Every migration date on this page follows the Atlas's own records, each drawn by its kind of date and by how it
      is set. An earlier version drew some of them as hard deadlines and gave years the records do not hold.
    </p>

    <h2 class="cm-subject">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="cm-sources">
      <li>NIST, FIPS 203, 204 and 205: post-quantum cryptography standards (August 2024).</li>
      <li>
        European Union: Directive (EU) 2022/2555 (NIS2); Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 (DORA); Regulation (EU) 2024/2847
        (Cyber Resilience Act), most of which applies from 11 December 2027.
      </li>
      <li>
        ENISA, the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity: cryptographic certification (EUCC) and post-quantum readiness
        surveys (with ISACA).
      </li>
      <li>
        NIS Cooperation Group: A Coordinated Implementation Roadmap for the Transition to Post-Quantum Cryptography
        (2025), a recommendation to Member States setting targets for the end of 2026, 2030 and 2035.
      </li>
      <li>BSI (Germany): technical guideline TR-02102 and its recommendations on the migration to post-quantum cryptography.</li>
      <li>
        United States: Executive Order 14412, Securing the Nation Against Advanced Cryptographic Attacks (22 June 2026),
        setting 2030 and 2031 for federal high value assets and high-impact systems; NSA Commercial National Security
        Algorithm Suite 2.0 (CNSA 2.0), with migration of national security systems targeted by 2035; Executive Order
        14306 (2025), amending Executive Order 14144.
      </li>
      <li>
        India: Department of Science and Technology, Implementation of Quantum Safe Ecosystem in India, Report of the Task
        Force (2026), and Report on Quantum-Safe Ecosystem in India, naming 2029 for critical information infrastructure
        and 2033 for enterprise-wide adoption; NITI Aayog, Roadmap for Transforming India into a Leading Quantum-Powered
        Economy, with a 2035 horizon.
      </li>
      <li>
        Thailand: NCSA QUANTA post-quantum readiness platform (pqc-learn.ncsa.or.th), a phased roadmap recommending the
        NIST standards, with high-priority systems migrated by 2028 and national completion by 2035.
      </li>
      <li>China: OSCCA SM-series national cryptographic algorithms.</li>
      <li>
        Industry qubit figures and targets: IBM (Condor, 1,121 qubits, 2023; a goal of 100,000 connected qubits by 2033);
        Microsoft (Majorana 1, eight topological qubits, 2025); Amazon (Ocelot, five data qubits and four ancilla qubits,
        2025).
      </li>
      <li>
        Qubits needed to break RSA-2048: Gidney, C., and Ekerå, M. (2019), 20 million noisy qubits; Gidney, C. (2025),
        under a million noisy qubits.
      </li>
      <li>
        ANSSI, BSI, the Netherlands NLNCSA and the Swedish National Communications Security Authority: joint position
        paper on quantum key distribution (January 2024), prioritising post-quantum migration.
      </li>
      <li>
        Global Risk Institute and evolutionQ: Quantum Threat Timeline Report (2024), and Mosca's risk inequality (x + y
        &gt; z).
      </li>
      <li>G7 Cyber Expert Group: statement on the risks of quantum computing to the financial sector (September 2024).</li>
      <li>Bradford, A. (2020). The Brussels Effect. Oxford University Press.</li>
      <li>
        Budde, B., and Konrad, K. (2019). Tentative governing of fuel cell innovation in a dynamic network of
        expectations. Research Policy, 48(5).
      </li>
      <li>CEPS: Pupillo, L., et al. (2025). Strengthening the EU Transition to a Quantum-Safe World, Task Force report.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cm-avail">
      The Atlas's data and code are public on <a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> and archived on Zenodo (DOI:
      <a href={ZENODO_URL} rel="noopener">{ZENODO_DOI}</a>). The profiles behind the chart:{' '}
      {
        withDates
          .map((a) => ({ a, href: profileHref(a.iso3) }))
          .filter((e) => e.href)
          .map((e, i, all) => <><a href={e.href!}>{e.a.name}</a>{i < all.length - 1 ? ', ' : '.'}</>)
      }
    </p>
    <p class="cm-attr">
      A commentary from the QSC Atlas, drawing on the CEPS Task Force on Strengthening the EU Transition to a
      Quantum-Safe World (2025).
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

